# RENAMING A USER METHOD MUST NOT CHANGE WHETHER THE PROGRAM SEGFAULTS.
#
# Two structurally identical halves, differing ONLY in what the `&self`
# mutator is called:
#
#     Listed.resize(&self)     -- `resize` WAS on the 25-entry
#                                 MUTATING_METHODS hand list
#     Unlisted.grow(&self)     -- `grow` was not
#
# The CoW prescan used to decide "does this call mutate its receiver" by
# testing the method's identifier text against that list, so the second half
# alone was rc 139 / ASan heap-use-after-free on BOTH backends while the
# first was correct (`todo/t0699`). The decision now comes from the semantic
# pass's typed per-receiver classifier
# (`semantic::safety::ReceiverMutations`), which cannot see a name.
#
# THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING: each mutator must push enough to force the
# backing store to reallocate (64x here), or the stale bind still points at a
# live buffer and the program is rc 0 under BOTH names, proving nothing.
#
# THE HEAP PAYLOAD IS NOT what makes the repro valid -- measured, a literal
# payload reproduces rc 139 just as well -- but `mk` is kept so the freed
# element is a heap String and ASan reports a clean heap-use-after-free in
# `gorget_string_clone_to_owned` rather than a bare SIGSEGV.
#
# RED-verified at the pre-fix compiler: rc 139 on C and on LLVM (the Unlisted
# half). Post-fix: `helloworld` twice on C, LLVM, the self-host lowerer lane and
# ggdef. ⚠ ggdef adjudicates VALUE semantics and is structurally blind to
# memory invalidation — it printed `helloworld` twice even pre-fix. The
# memory-validity adjudicator for this class is ASan, and it has its own cell:
# `tests/fixtures/security/sound_user_mutator_name_invariant_uaf.gg`.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct Listed:
    Vector[String] items

equip Listed:
    void resize(&self):
        int i = 0
        while i < 64:
            self.items.push(mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
            i = i + 1

    String probe(&self):
        String s = self.items.get(0).unwrap()
        self.resize()
        return s

struct Unlisted:
    Vector[String] items

equip Unlisted:
    void grow(&self):
        int i = 0
        while i < 64:
            self.items.push(mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
            i = i + 1

    String probe(&self):
        String s = self.items.get(0).unwrap()
        self.grow()
        return s

void main():
    Listed a = Listed([mk("hello", "world")])
    print(a.probe())
    Unlisted b = Unlisted([mk("hello", "world")])
    print(b.probe())
